Let $Q$ be any Sylow $p$-subgroup of $H$. Since $Q$ is a $p$-subgroup of $G$, it is contained in some Sylow $p$-subgroup $P_1$ of $G$. By uniqueness, $P_1=P$, so $Q\le P\cap H$. Thus $P\cap H$ contains a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $H$. Because $P\cap H$ is itself a $p$-subgroup of $H$, it must equal $Q$. Therefore $P\cap H$ is the unique Sylow $p$-subgroup of $H$.
