\begin{proof}
Normality of $P$ implies that $P$ is the unique Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$, since all Sylow $p$-subgroups are conjugate in $G$.

The intersection $P\cap H$ is a $p$-subgroup of $H$. Let $Q$ be a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $H$. By the Sylow containment theorem, $Q$ is contained in a Sylow $p$-subgroup $R$ of $G$. Since $P$ is unique in $G$, we have $R=P$, and hence $Q\le P\cap H$.

As $Q$ is maximal among $p$-subgroups of $H$ and $P\cap H$ is a $p$-subgroup containing $Q$, it follows that $P\cap H=Q$. Consequently, every Sylow $p$-subgroup $Q$ of $H$ equals $P\cap H$, proving uniqueness.
