Because $P\trianglelefteq G$ and $P$ is a Sylow $p$-subgroup, it is the unique Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$. Indeed, every Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$ is conjugate to $P$, while every conjugate of $P$ is $P$ itself.

Set $K=P\cap H$. Then $K$ is a $p$-subgroup of $H$. Choose a Sylow $p$-subgroup $Q$ of $H$. The subgroup $Q$ is a $p$-subgroup of $G$, so it lies in a Sylow $p$-subgroup $R$ of $G$. Uniqueness in $G$ gives $R=P$, and therefore $Q\le K$.

Since $K$ is a $p$-subgroup of $H$ containing the Sylow $p$-subgroup $Q$, maximality of $Q$ gives $K=Q$. Thus $P\cap H$ is a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $H$. The same argument applies to every Sylow $p$-subgroup of $H$, so all of them equal $P\cap H$.
\end{proof}

