Let $Q$ be a Sylow $p$-subgroup of $H$. Since $P$ is normal in $G$ and is Sylow, it is the unique Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$: any Sylow $p$-subgroup of $G$ is conjugate to $P$, and normality gives $gPg^{-1}=P$ for every $g\in G$.

The subgroup $Q$ is a $p$-subgroup of $G$, so it is contained in some Sylow $p$-subgroup $R$ of $G$. By uniqueness, $R=P$, hence $Q\le P\cap H$. Since $P\cap H$ is itself a $p$-subgroup of $H$, and $Q$ is maximal among such subgroups, we obtain $P\cap H=Q$.

